(>> 3.1 Role, Architecture and Evolutionary Types of Operating Systems) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(An Operating System \(OS\) is core system software that acts as an intermediary between) Tj
T*
(computer hardware and user application programs, managing hardware resources) Tj
T*
(efficiently:) Tj
T*
(* Core Objectives: Resource abstraction \(hiding hardware complexity\), Resource) Tj
T*
(allocation \(CPU, memory, storage, I/O devices\), and Protection/Security.) Tj
T*
(* Evolutionary Paradigms: Batch Systems \(jobs grouped without interaction\),) Tj
T*
(Multiprogramming \(keeping CPU busy by running another job when one waits for I/O\),) Tj
T*
(Time-Sharing \(multitasking via rapid CPU context switching\), Distributed Systems, and) Tj
T*
(Real-Time Operating Systems \(RTOS - strict deadline guarantees: Hard RTOS vs Soft) Tj
T*
(RTOS\).) Tj
T*
(* Dual-Mode Operation and Hardware Protection:) Tj
T*
(- User Mode \(Mode bit = 1\): Application software executes with restricted privileges;) Tj
T*
(cannot execute privileged machine instructions or access hardware registers directly.) Tj
T*
(- Kernel Mode / Supervisor Mode \(Mode bit = 0\): Unrestricted hardware access. Traps and) Tj
T*
(System Calls transition CPU from User Mode to Kernel Mode.) Tj
T*
(* OS Structural Models: Monolithic Kernel \(Linux - all OS services reside in kernel) Tj
T*
(space; maximum performance\) vs Microkernel \(Mach, QNX - only IPC, basic scheduling,) Tj
T*
(and virtual memory in kernel; file systems and drivers run as user space daemons;) Tj
T*
(high reliability and fault isolation\).) Tj
T*
